WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
Every protein in the nucleus was made in the cytoplasm.

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:13.020
Histones, polymerases and transcription factors all have to be brought in.

3
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.040
The border is busy in both directions at once.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.300
So how does the nucleus collect the right proteins, keep them, and make traffic run one way?

5
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:28.763
The gates in this border are nuclear pore complexes.

6
00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:31.563
Anyone small can stroll across.

7
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:38.183
A large protein needs a passport, a short signal called a nuclear localization

8
00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:42.763
signal. A courier called importin α reads it.

9
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:50.763
Importin β binds importin α, then moves the complex through the pore by binding FG repeats.

10
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.907
The pore does not set the direction.

11
00:00:54.727 --> 00:00:57.167
The couriers do not push anyone through.

12
00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:03.307
What makes traffic one way is a rule about where couriers put their loads down.

13
00:01:04.247 --> 00:01:08.027
That rule depends on Ran, a small GTPase.

14
00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:12.967
Ran is on with GTP and off with GDP.

15
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:22.510
In the nucleus, an exchange factor called RCC1 sits on chromatin and loads GTP onto

16
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.630
Ran. So RanGTP is made in the nucleus.

17
00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:36.510
In the cytoplasm, RanGAP1 makes Ran break down its GTP, so cytoplasmic RanGTP

18
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:45.410
stays low. A carrier called NTF2 brings RanGDP back into the nucleus to be recharged.

19
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:48.203
The couriers read this gradient.

20
00:01:48.903 --> 00:01:56.543
An importin loads cargo where RanGTP is low, then drops it when RanGTP binds in

21
00:01:56.543 --> 00:02:01.563
the nucleus. An exportin such as CRM1 does the opposite.

22
00:02:02.303 --> 00:02:05.703
It binds its cargo only together with RanGTP.

23
00:02:06.563 --> 00:02:10.043
In the cytoplasm, hydrolysis breaks the complex apart.

24
00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:13.023
Where is the energy spent?

25
00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:20.703
Not on pushing. In a test tube assay, transportin, another importin, carried cargo

26
00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:24.203
across pores with no Ran or nucleotide present.

27
00:02:25.303 --> 00:02:31.403
GTP hydrolysis builds and resets the gradient that decides where loads are picked up

28
00:02:31.403 --> 00:02:38.283
and dropped. Keep this rule: the pore is open, and RanGTP sets the direction.
